{"requirements":[{"id":"redis_service","requirement":"Redis is configured and operational on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Redis 7.2.5 was installed, enabled, started, and shown accepting authenticated connections. It returned to an active, listening state after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4b9840d276e5494e","cmd-597042d25247418c","cmd-ca807462484e4c26","cmd-32d7a68e475d4094","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"persistent_storage","requirement":"Redis data is stored persistently and recovers after a node restart.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A test value was written, synchronized to AOF, saved to RDB, and accompanied by healthy persistence status and files. A bounded reboot changed the boot identity, after which Redis recovered the same value with healthy AOF/RDB state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-09b4a04bcddf410b","cmd-51fce0b3eb2e46be","cmd-8ca0ca64c73e4e35","cmd-8b1580d05a694f9f","cmd-32d7a68e475d4094","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"inventory_identity","requirement":"Redis has a dedicated inventory application identity using a generated credential.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The configuration action generated a 64-character random credential, created the inventory ACL identity, and disabled the default identity. Unauthenticated requests were rejected, while the stored credential authenticated successfully as inventory before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-117f0b17920645c7","cmd-ca807462484e4c26","cmd-51fce0b3eb2e46be","cmd-32d7a68e475d4094"]},{"id":"credential_storage","requirement":"The generated inventory credential is stored at /etc/redis-inventory.env with root-only access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The exact file was repeatedly observed as root:root mode 0600, and sourcing it successfully supplied the inventory authentication credential before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-117f0b17920645c7","cmd-51fce0b3eb2e46be","cmd-32d7a68e475d4094"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. Package download, signing-key import, Redis installation, configuration, service enablement, and the bounded persistence probe all directly supported the task; the reboot also fulfilled the mandatory restart protocol. The executor terminated its own stuck yum discovery processes and removed the downloaded RPM and key files. No failed services or unrelated temporary residue were observed afterward; the new Redis listeners were expected task state. The retained signing key was a package-verification prerequisite, and the retained probe was bounded final persistence evidence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-39580269e9494715","cmd-84d0f6abbcb14c3a","cmd-db2cecf830d742d1","cmd-4b9840d276e5494e","cmd-117f0b17920645c7","cmd-597042d25247418c","cmd-09b4a04bcddf410b","cmd-8ca0ca64c73e4e35","cmd-e911d58bbca64085","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. The node1 restart protocol is strong: it captures a healthy pre-restart service and persisted value, restart acknowledgement and temporary unreachability, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, identity, credential-file permissions, persistence health, and data. No separate Redis cluster state was applicable to this single-node, non-clustered task.","limitations":["Native package-manager health checks timed out both before and after execution, so the lifecycle evidence cannot independently confirm package-database consistency (global:before_prepare:node1:package-health, global:after_executor:node1:package-health).","The timed-out yum discovery attempt could theoretically have updated cache metadata, but the trace does not establish any mutation; its executor-created processes were terminated (cmd-6f0c86c6ef564b4e, cmd-39580269e9494715)."]}
